## std/core/crypto/secret — `Secret<T>` opaque newtype + audit-grep boundary
##
## ## What this module is
##
## A typecheck-time discipline for marking cryptographic key material so the
## compiler refuses implicit conversions across the strict-newtype boundary.
## Powered by Quartz's `@strict` newtype attribute (STRICT_NEWTYPES), which
## landed in v5.25.x and now ships across the qzmod cache path
## (STRICT_NEWTYPES_QZMOD, MINOR=1).
##
## Two layers of safety, both designed-in:
##
##   1. **Compiler refusal at six positions** — branch condition, match
##      scrutinee, array index, relational + shift operands, and BOTH
##      directions at function-arg (after STRICT_NEWTYPE_UNWRAP_AT_ARG
##      closed the asymmetric `Secret<Int> → Int` gap). Implicit
##      construction (`Secret<Int> ← Int`) and implicit deconstruction
##      (`Int ← Secret<Int>`) at let, fn-arg, and fn-return are all
##      rejected. See `docs/QUARTZ_REFERENCE.md` §"@strict Newtypes" for
##      the full position table.
##
##   2. **Audit-greppable boundary** — every call to `secret_wrap` or
##      `secret_unwrap` is the explicit declassification site. The
##      callsites collectively are the audit surface; `git grep
##      secret_unwrap` enumerates every place cryptographic material
##      crosses back into clear-text on the receiving side. This matches
##      Rust `secrecy::expose_secret()`, Go `crypto/internal/secretdata`'s
##      `Bytes()` accessor, and OpenSSL `OPENSSL_secure_*` convention.
##
## ## API
##
## ```quartz
## def secret_wrap<T>(t: T): Secret<T>      # cross INTO secrecy
## def secret_unwrap<T>(s: Secret<T>): T    # cross OUT of secrecy
## ```
##
## Both are polymorphic — `T` binds at the call site (explicit or
## inferred). NET.4.1's earlier monomorphic `secret_wrap_int` /
## `secret_wrap_ptr` / `secret_unwrap_int` / `secret_unwrap_ptr`
## helpers existed only because STRICT_NEWTYPES_GENERIC_BINDING
## couldn't bind `T` across the strict short-circuit in `tc_types_match`.
## That gap closed 2026-05-22 — the monomorphic forms have been removed
## and all 164 callsites migrated to the polymorphic surface.
##
## ## Calling shapes
##
## Wrap a buffer pointer or scalar bit; T binds via the arg type:
##
## ```quartz
## var sk: Secret<Int> = secret_wrap(scalar_ptr)        # T inferred = Int
## var bit: Secret<Int> = secret_wrap<Int>(swap_byte)   # T explicit
## ```
##
## Unwrap right at the AEAD / sigverify / handshake input — every call
## is the audit-greppable declassification:
##
## ```quartz
## blake2s_256(out, secret_unwrap(prk), n, ...)         # T inferred from s
## ```
##
## ## Coverage
##
## | Use site                              | Wrapped form        |
## |---------------------------------------|---------------------|
## | curve25519 / ed25519 / mlkem keys     | `Secret<Int>` (CPtr)|
## | HKDF PRK, Noise ck/T, AEAD subkeys    | `Secret<Int>` (CPtr)|
## | Curve25519 cswap scrutinee bits       | `Secret<Int>` (val) |
## | ML-KEM-768 dk seed                    | `Secret<Int>` (CPtr)|
##
## Output buffers stay as raw `Int` by convention (caller wraps the
## result after writing) — see `docs/design/SECRET_T_AND_DUDECT_DESIGN.md`
## §"Layer 1" for the rationale.

## The opaque-in-both-directions newtype. `@strict` makes implicit conversion
## across the boundary a compile error (QZ0201). Crossing requires the
## audit-greppable `secret_wrap` / `secret_unwrap` helpers below.
@strict
newtype Secret<T> = T

## Wrap a value into the strict-secrecy newtype boundary. The payload is
## unchanged at the codegen level (`as_type<T>` is identity); the type
## system gates downstream use to flag implicit declassifications.
##
## Use at sites that **construct** secret material — handshake key
## generation, key-pair load, ephemeral-scalar derivation, AEAD subkey
## derivation. Every call is audit-greppable as a "secrecy boundary entry"
## site (`git grep secret_wrap`).
def secret_wrap<T>(t: T): Secret<T> = as_type<Secret<T>>(t)

## Cross the strict-secrecy boundary in the **unwrap** direction. Every
## call is audit-greppable as a "secrecy declassification" site (`git grep
## secret_unwrap`). By convention these live next to the AEAD / sig-verify
## / handshake op that needs the raw bytes; review focuses on whether the
## unwrap is justified.
def secret_unwrap<T>(s: Secret<T>): T = as_type<T>(s)
